  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Oak Island has swallowed 230 years of treasure hunting. Nova Scotia, 1795: a dip in the ground and three teenagers with shovels.",
        "visual_query": "nova scotia coastal island forest",
        "visual_fallback": "coastal island"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Ten feet down, flagstones. Below that, oak platforms every ten feet, all the way to ninety. Then the shaft flooded and the digging stopped.",
        "visual_query": "nova scotia shoreline spruce trees",
        "visual_fallback": "dug earth"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Every generation since has tried again. Pumps, drills, a causeway built in 1965 just to haul heavy machinery across. Nothing.",
        "visual_query": "heavy excavation machinery dig site",
        "visual_fallback": "digging machinery"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So what is actually down there? Geologists point at the bedrock: limestone and gypsum, the rock that dissolves into caves and then caves in.",
        "visual_query": "limestone cave gypsum rock formation",
        "visual_fallback": "limestone cave"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1878 a farmer's oxen crashed through the ground 120 yards away, into a natural cavern. The money pit was probably a sinkhole all along.",
        "visual_query": "nova scotia farmland sinkhole ground",
        "visual_fallback": "collapsed ground"
      }
    ],
